William Galston's 'most fundamental objection to [Van Parijs' articulation of]
UBI'#an[2], i.e. Universal Basic Income --- a regular, subsistence level income
payed unconditionally to all members of a society#an[1] --- is _reciprocity_.
He believes that 'people who receive benefits should make contributions --- if
they are able'#an[2], and is concerned that a UBI would enable, even encourage,
people to undercut this tenet of societal justice so is morally indefensible.

Van Parijs' own (rather practical) considerations concerning the problem of
reciprocity are addressed by Galston, and to great effect, I believe, so I
shall not dwell on them here. Goodin's alternative response is to suggest a UBI
caveated by a requirement for contribution#an[3] --- i.e. not really UBI
proper since it is not unconditional. I see this as a lazy entreating of
criticism and so will show that no such contribution requirement which might
illustrate cases of justice or injustice could be comprehensively articulated,
due primarily to complications introduced by Galston's emphasis on time.
Considered besides Van Parijs' practical arguments for UBI, Galston's
philosophical qualms are then overturned.

Firstly, it is worth addressing Galston's note that somebody had ought to
contribute 'if they are able'. He too observes, if briefly, the vagueness of
this qualification, and so I shall herein assume an understanding that we are
primarily discussing ordinary, healthy adults, as it is these people that are
most capable of contributing, and thus potentially most immoral for failing to
do so. Therefore, if I can show that reciprocity is not applicable for this
group, then it seems to follow rather directly that the same must be true of
those less able.

A frequently bandied around example of one who is all take and no give (and
thus a contravenor of reciprocity) is the so-called Malibu surfer#an[1], who
abandons work to pursue selfish pleasure by shredding waves off Malibu beach.
It is then taken as given that this individual is doing something immoral, in
particular: behaviour that should not be provided for by society. I feel that
this example clarifies the sense in which Galston uses the term reciprocity, in
two key ways.

\1. Galston fails to identify any sense of order in his understanding of
reciprocity, in terms of whether one had ought to contribute to society in
order to _earn_ provision (as with a state pension), or be provided for to
facilitate contributing (as with a UBI), despite both orders being forms of
reciprocity.  Galston suggests that the latter, which I will term _backward
reciprocity_, would degenerate into provision for idle non-contributors, and so
is unjust. So, to clarify, rather than argue that this degeneration would not
take place (as Van Parijs does#an[1]), I will instead argue that the former,
which I will term _forward reciprocity_, also degenerates in such a myriad of
ways as to nullify any perceived association with justice.

\2. Galston understands contribution in a consequentialist sense. If a surfer
surfs with the intention of thereby enacting peace on Earth, they clearly have
virtuous intentions, and yet a lack of impact is the criterion on which they
are judged. Indeed, if we #jlb were to caveat any UBI with the requirement of
good intention, we would be considering a bureaucracy of such absurd proportion
and expense that I feel this practical impossibility is enough to dispel the
notion. Therefore, I will focus on Galston's consequentialist articulation of
contribution.

Having now explicated more clearly the criteria of Galston's reciprocity, we
stand in better stead to see why the delineation of _socially useful
contributions_ (to borrow Van Parijs' term#an[1]) from idleness does not
produce a just criterion for earning provision.

Time is a key characteristic of Galston's argument, insofar as his perception
of socially useful contribution can only be understood retrospectively, by
analysing it's impact and rewarding accordingly. This introduction of time
proves a curse for forward reciprocity, however, as time eludes control.

Van Gogh is widely exalted as a bastion of profound cultural value. In his day,
however, he was seen merely as a mental asylum patient busy misunderstanding
the impressionists to the detriment of many a canvas#an[5,6]. This hardly
sounds like an individual partaking in socially useful contributions and that
was indeed the consensus of many of his contemporaries. Thus, although his
contributions have come to be regarded as socially useful, they have taken too
long to do so. Therefore, though Van Gogh adheres to Galston's reciprocity, the
recognition thereof fails to proffer the deserved benefit, which hardly sounds
like justice to me.

Of course, Galston may well present the inverse case: somebody who thinks
themself a future Van Gogh, brimming with potential, but who ends up producing
little of any worth. To provide provision for this individual is seen, by
Galston, as also unjust. These injustices are perhaps analogous to false
imprisonment and false release. Galston neglects to clarify which injustice is
the greater, and I don't think the answer thereunto is trivial. This
inconclusivity frustrates Galston's clean divide between forward reciprocity
being just and backward reciprocity not so.

Pateman illustrates a similar case, wherein present social utility is massively
underrecognised, if it all: house and care work. This labour, the burden of
which is disproportionately lain on women, is no doubt vital and yet is
devalued due to the patriachal structure of our society#an[7]. Pateman notes
that the literature is pervaded by oversight of this issue, even by Van Parijs,
who seems to dismiss the lack of recognition as unrelated to widespread
bias#an[1,7].

Galston does note that he believes this problem to be addressable by other
means, namely fiddling the tax system in favour of carers, but this approach
necessarily reduces such work to being lesser than full-time employment,
thus failing to succintly demarcate house and care work as truly valuable, and
so compounding this fundamentally social issue by preserving the biases from
which it has evolved. This oversight is, to my mind, again the fault of time.
Whomever judges the social utility of a contribution does so from their point
in time and it's beliefs. Thus Van Gogh was judged as merely 'insane'#an[5],
and those that do care work are judged as _merely_ doing care work. This
subjectivity challenges any objective conception of reciprocity forward or
backward, and to suppose ourselves capable of objective oversight is, I
believe, naïve. To further exemplify this, we turn to Graeber, who rallied
against the extent to which present social structure defines utility, rather
than any objective considerations.

What Graeber terms 'bullshit jobs' are jobs that exist to serve a wholly
contrived purpose#an[4]. Rather than endless fruitless labour, he suggests we
eliminate the purpose, and the job finds itself forever done. He provides the
example of a charity worker who helps worthy means-tested benefit applicants
fill out their applications in a manner advantageous to getting their case
successfully recognised --- necessitated by the miserably bureaucratic process
of application that perplexes many applicants. Though seemingly morally
upstanding, this role exists to combat a moral barrier imposed by an
arbitrarily contrived machine of society. Means-tested benefits could be
replaced with a less fraught system (such as UBI), and the need for righting a
wrong would disappear, as the wrong is permenantly resolved.

Fundamentally, these jobs do have a positive social impact, and so are judged
socially useful, but the positivity is relevant only to society constructed
around abstractions (forward reciprocal justice, in the above example), and not
to society as a set of real people who have real needs (access to the means to
basic survival, in the above example).

Controversially similar cases are perhaps those in the financial sector, or
even you and I --- sat around idly pondering the moral worth of economic
policy. None of us may think ourselves useless, but Graeber argues that this
is merely due to having been told that we are not. Through each of these cases,
I have aimed to make clear that to get any purchase on some notion of social
utility (not even a perfect one, but merely a sufficiently good one), we would
have to act as omniscient observers, detached from time and society.
Unfortunately, such observers are not readily available, and therefore no clear
definition could be formulated.

UBI is also challenged by practical difficulties (and very slippery ones at
that), but if one concerns oneself solely with the most significant moral
objection, that is a need for reciprocal justice, then I have shown avoiding
UBI in favour of forward reciprocity is no more effective for this end than
instituting UBI with a warm regard for backward reciprocity. Therefore,
Galston's objection fails to articulate how a UBI would be any more unjust than
present society. Viewing this conclusion alongside the numerous practical
benefits of a UBI (of which Van Parijs articulates many#an[1]), I concur with
Van Parijs: UBI is the morally best choice for the government, and they lack a
philosophical excuse to not iron out those aforesaid difficulties and bring UBI
to life.
